Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE & MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE

The year is 1843. London is a city of fog and biting cold.

NARRATOR
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Scrooge signed the register of his burial. And Scrooge's name was good upon 'Change. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

A sign above a warehouse door reads: "SCROOGE AND MARLEY."

NARRATOR
Scrooge knew he was dead. Of course he did. Scrooge and he were partners for many years. Scrooge was his sole executor, his sole friend, and sole mourner. And even Scrooge was not so dreadfully cut up by the sad event, but that he was an excellent man of business on the very day of the funeral.

Inside the COUNTING-HOUSE, EBENEZER SCROOGE, a thin, flinty man, sits at his desk. He wears a frosty rime on his head and eyebrows. His clerk, BOB CRATCHIT, shivers in a dismal cell beyond, copying letters. Bob's fire is a single coal. Scrooge keeps the coal-box in his own room.

A cheerful young man, FRED, Scrooge's nephew, bursts in. He glows from the cold, his face ruddy.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

Scrooge has no ready answer.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug.

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? Merry Christmas! Out upon merry Christmas! What's Christmas time to you but a time for paying bills without money; a time for finding yourself a year older, but not an hour richer? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with 'Merry Christmas' on his lips, should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart. He should!

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
Keep it! But you don't keep it.

SCROOGE
Let me leave it alone, then. Much good may it do you! Much good it has ever done you!

FRED
There are many things from which I might have derived good, by which I have not profited, I dare say. Christmas among the rest. But I am sure I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time; the only time I know of, in the long calendar of the year, when men and women seem by one consent to open their shut-up hearts freely. And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good, and will do me good; and I say, God bless it!

Bob Cratchit involuntarily applauds. Scrooge glares at him. Bob pokes his tiny fire, extinguishing its last spark.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation! You're quite a powerful speaker, sir. I wonder you don't go into Parliament.

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
I would see you in that extremity first.

FRED
But why? Why?

SCROOGE
Why did you get married?

FRED
Because I fell in love.

SCROOGE
Because you fell in love! Good afternoon!

FRED
Nay, uncle, but you never came to see me before that happened. Why give it as a reason for not coming now?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I am sorry, with all my heart, to find you so resolute. But I have made the trial in homage to Christmas, and I'll keep my Christmas humour to the last. So A Merry Christmas, uncle!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
And A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves. He stops at the outer door to wish Bob a Merry Christmas. Bob returns the greeting cordially.

SCROOGE
(Muttering)
There's another fellow; my clerk, with fifteen shillings a week, and a wife and family, talking about a merry Christmas. I'll retire to Bedlam.

Two portly gentlemen, MR. WITHERS and MR. POOLE, enter. They carry books and papers.

MR. WITHERS
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

MR. WITHERS
We have no doubt his liberality is well represented by his surviving partner.

Scrooge frowns at the word "liberality" and hands back their credentials.

MR. WITHERS
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute, who suffer greatly at the present time. Many thousands are in want of common necessaries; hundreds of thousands are in want of common comforts, sir.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

MR. WITHERS
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

MR. WITHERS
They are. Still, I wish I could say they were not.

SCROOGE
The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

MR. WITHERS
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid, from what you said at first, that something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it.

MR. WITHERS
Under the impression that they scarcely furnish Christian cheer of mind or body to the multitude, a few of us are endeavouring to raise a fund to buy the Poor some meat and drink, and means of warmth. We choose this time, because it is a time, of all others, when Want is keenly felt, and Abundance rejoices. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

MR. WITHERS
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. Since you ask me what I wish, gentlemen, that is my answer. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. I help to support the establishments I have mentioned--they cost enough; and those who are badly off must go there.

MR. POOLE
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. Besides--excuse me--I don't know that.

MR. POOLE
But you might know it.

SCROOGE
It's not my business. It's enough for a man to understand his own business, and not to interfere with other people's. Mine occupies me constantly. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The collectors withdraw, defeated. Scrooge resumes his work, a smug look on his face.

NARRATOR
The fog and darkness thickened. The cold became intense. A YOUNG SINGER stooped down at Scrooge's keyhole to regale him with a Christmas carol.

A young YOUNG SINGER'S voice is heard, thin but sweet.

YOUNG SINGER (O.S.)
God bless you, merry gentleman! May nothing you dismay!

Scrooge seizes a ruler. The YOUNG SINGER yelps and flees.

Later, the hour of shutting up the counting-house arrives. Scrooge dismounts from his stool. Bob Cratchit snuffs out his candle and puts on his hat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. If I was to stop half-a-crown for it, you'd think yourself ill-used, I'll be bound?

Bob smiles faintly.

SCROOGE
And yet, you don't think me ill-used, when I pay a day's wages for no work.

BOB CRATCHIT
It was only once a year, sir.

SCROOGE
A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob promises he will. Scrooge walks out with a growl. Bob, with his white comforter dangling, slides down Cornhill with a group of boys, then runs home.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Scrooge eats a melancholy dinner alone at a tavern, then goes home. He lives in chambers that once belonged to Marley. The yard is dark, the fog and frost thick.

Scrooge inserts his key into the lock. The door knocker, large and old, transforms. It is JACOB MARLEY'S face. It is not angry, but looks at Scrooge as Marley used to look, with ghostly spectacles on its forehead. Its hair stirs, its eyes are wide and motionless.

Scrooge stares. The face becomes a knocker again. He puts his hand on the key, turns it, and enters. He pauses, looking cautiously behind the door. Nothing.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

He slams the door. The sound echoes like thunder through the house. Scrooge fastens the door, walks across the hall, and slowly up the stairs, trimming his candle.

NARRATOR
You might have got a hearse up that staircase, and taken it broadwise. There was plenty of width for that.

Scrooge reaches his rooms. He walks through them, checking everything. Sitting-room, bedroom, lumber-room. All as they should be. A small fire in the grate, gruel on the hob.

Satisfied, he double-locks his door, which is not his custom. He takes off his cravat, puts on his dressing-gown, slippers, and nightcap, and sits by the fire to eat his gruel. The fire is very low.

The fireplace is paved with quaint Dutch tiles illustrating scriptures. Cains and Abels, Pharaoh's daughters, Queens of Sheba. But Marley's face, seven years dead, comes like a rod and swallows up the whole.

SCROOGE
Humbug!

He walks across the room, then sits again. His glance falls on a disused bell. It begins to swing, softly at first, then loudly. Every bell in the house rings. The bells cease. A clanking noise rises from below, as if a heavy chain is being dragged. It comes up the stairs, then straight to his door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

The heavy door opens. JACOB MARLEY'S GHOST, transparent, enters. He wears his pigtail, waistcoat, tights, and boots. A long chain, made of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, and heavy purses, is clasped about his middle.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then? You're particular, for a shade.

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you--can you sit down?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I can.

SCROOGE
Do it, then.

The Ghost sits on the opposite side of the fireplace.

MARLEY'S GHOST
You don't believe in me.

SCROOGE
I don't.

MARLEY'S GHOST
What evidence would you have of my reality beyond that of your senses?

SCROOGE
I don't know.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Why do you doubt your senses?

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects them. A slight disorder of the stomach makes them cheats. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

Scrooge tries to distract himself, but the Ghost's voice disturbs him. The Ghost's hair, skirts, and tassels are agitated as by hot vapour.

SCROOGE
You see this toothpick?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I do.

SCROOGE
You are not looking at it.

MARLEY'S GHOST
But I see it, notwithstanding.

SCROOGE
Well! I have but to swallow this, and be for the rest of my days persecuted by a legion of goblins, all of my own creation. Humbug, I tell you! Humbug!

The Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain. Scrooge clutches his chair. The phantom takes off a bandage around its head, and its lower jaw drops upon its breast.

Scrooge falls to his knees, clasping his hands before his face.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Man of the worldly mind! Do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen, and travel far and wide; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death. It is doomed to wander through the world--oh, woe is me!--and witness what it cannot share, but might have shared on earth, and turned to happiness!

The spectre raises another cry, shakes its chain, and wrings its shadowy hands.

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard; I girded it on of my own free will, and of my own free will I wore it. Is its pattern strange to you?

Scrooge trembles.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it, since. It is a ponderous chain!

Scrooge glances at the floor, expecting to see his own chain.

SCROOGE
Jacob, Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY'S GHOST
I have none to give. It comes from other regions, Ebenezer Scrooge, and is conveyed by other ministers, to other kinds of men. My spirit never walked beyond our counting-house--mark me!--in life my spirit never roved beyond the narrow limits of our money-changing hole; and weary journeys lie before me!

Scrooge, still on his knees, puts his hands in his breeches pockets.

SCROOGE
You must have been very slow about it, Jacob.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Slow!

SCROOGE
Seven years dead. And travelling all the time!

MARLEY'S GHOST
The whole time. No rest, no peace. Incessant torture of remorse.

SCROOGE
You travel fast?

MARLEY'S GHOST
On the wings of the wind.

SCROOGE
You might have got over a great quantity of ground in seven years.

The Ghost sets up another cry and clanks its chain hideously.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Oh! captive, bound, and double-ironed, not to know, that ages of incessant labour by immortal creatures, for this earth must pass into eternity before the good of which it is susceptible is all developed. Not to know that no space of regret can make amends for one life's opportunity misused! Yet such was I! Oh! such was I!

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business. The dealings of my trade were but a drop of water in the comprehensive ocean of my business!

The Ghost holds up its chain, then flings it heavily to the ground.

MARLEY'S GHOST
At this time of the rolling year, I suffer most. Why did I walk through crowds of fellow-beings with my eyes turned down, and never raise them to that blessed Star which led the Wise Men to a poor abode! Were there no poor homes to which its light would have conducted me!

Scrooge quakes.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Hear me! My time is nearly gone.

SCROOGE
I will. But don't be hard upon me! Don't be flowery, Jacob! Pray!

MARLEY'S GHOST
How it is that I appear before you in a shape that you can see, I may not tell. I have sat invisible beside you many and many a day.

Scrooge shivers, wiping perspiration from his brow.

MARLEY'S GHOST
That is no light part of my penance. I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. A chance and hope of my procuring, Ebenezer.

SCROOGE
You were always a good friend to me. Thank'ee!

MARLEY'S GHOST
You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

Scrooge's face falls.

SCROOGE
Is that the chance and hope you mentioned, Jacob?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is.

SCROOGE
I--I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One.

SCROOGE
Couldn't I take 'em all at once, and have it over, Jacob?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

The spectre takes its head-wrapper from the table and binds it around its head. Scrooge hears the smart sound of its teeth. He raises his eyes. The apparition walks backward. The window raises itself.

Confused noises, lamentations, and wailings fill the air. The spectre joins in the mournful dirge and floats out into the bleak night. Scrooge follows to the window.

The air is filled with phantoms, wandering, moaning, all wearing chains. Many were known to Scrooge in life. One old ghost, with an iron safe attached to its ankle, cries piteously, unable to assist a wretched woman with an infant on a doorstep below. The spirits seek to interfere for good, but have lost the power forever.

The phantoms and their voices fade. The night becomes as it was. Scrooge closes the window. He examines the door; it is double-locked. He tries to say "Humbug!" but stops. Exhausted, he goes to bed without undressing and falls asleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes in darkness. A church bell strikes the four quarters, then counts to twelve.

SCROOGE
Why, it isn't possible that I can have slept through a whole day and far into another night.

He scrambles out of bed, gropes to the window, and rubs frost off. It is still foggy and cold. He returns to bed, perplexed. He remembers Marley's warning. He resolves to stay awake.

The chimes sound again.

SCROOGE
(Counting)
A quarter past. Half-past! A quarter to it. The hour itself, and nothing else!

The hour bell sounds a deep, dull, hollow ONE. Light flashes. The bed curtains are drawn aside by a hand. SCROOGE, sitting up, faces an unearthly visitor.

It is the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST, a strange figure, like a child yet like an old man. Its hair is white, its face unwrinkled. It wears a white tunic trimmed with summer flowers, and a lustrous belt. From its head springs a bright jet of light. It holds a branch of green holly and a great extinguisher for a cap. The figure fluctuates, now distinct, now dissolving.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

Scrooge asks the Spirit to cover its light with its cap.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What! Would you so soon put out, with worldly hands, the light I give? Is it not enough that you are one of those whose passions made this cap, and force me through whole trains of years to wear it low upon my brow!

Scrooge disclaims offense and asks its business.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your welfare! Your reclamation, then. Take heed!

The Ghost puts out its strong hand and clasps Scrooge gently by the arm.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Rise! and walk with me!

Scrooge protests the weather and his attire. The grasp is irresistible. He rises. The Spirit moves towards the window. Scrooge clasps its robe.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

The Spirit lays its hand on Scrooge's heart. They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

They stand on an open country road, fields on either hand. The city, darkness, and mist have vanished. It is a clear, cold, winter day with snow.

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

The Spirit gazes mildly. Scrooge is conscious of a thousand forgotten odors.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your lip is trembling. And what is that upon your cheek?

SCROOGE
(Muttering)
A pimple.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
You recollect the way?

SCROOGE
Remember it! I could walk it blindfold.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Strange to have forgotten it for so many years! Let us go on.

They walk along the road. Scrooge recognizes every gate, post, and tree. A market-town appears. Shaggy ponies with boys, country gigs and carts, all full of merry music.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us.

Scrooge knows and names the travelers. His cold eye glistens. He is filled with gladness as they wish each other Merry Christmas.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

Scrooge sobs.

INT. OLD SCHOOLHOUSE - DAY

They enter a dreary hall, then a long, bare, melancholy room. A lonely boy, YOUNG SCROOGE, reads near a feeble fire. Scrooge sits on a form and weeps.

The Spirit touches his arm, pointing to Young Scrooge. A MAN in foreign garments, ALI BABA, stands outside the window, axe in belt, leading an ass laden with wood.

SCROOGE
Why, it's Ali Baba! It's dear old honest Ali Baba! Yes, yes, I know! One Christmas time, when yonder solitary child was left here all alone, he did come, for the first time, just like that. Poor boy! And Valentine, and his wild brother, Orson; there they go! And what's his name, who was put down in his drawers, asleep, at the Gate of Damascus; don't you see him! And the Sultan's Groom turned upside down by the Genii; there he is upon his head! Serve him right. I'm glad of it. What business had he to be married to the Princess!

Scrooge laughs and cries.

SCROOGE
There's the Parrot! Green body and yellow tail, with a thing like a lettuce growing out of the top of his head; there he is! Poor Robin Crusoe, he called him, when he came home again after sailing round the island. 'Poor Robin Crusoe, where have you been, Robin Crusoe?' The man thought he was dreaming, but he wasn't. It was the Parrot, you know. There goes Friday, running for his life to the little creek! Halloa! Hoop! Halloo!

Scrooge's excitement fades to pity.

SCROOGE
Poor boy!

He cries again. He mutters, drying his eyes.

SCROOGE
I wish... but it's too late now.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What is the matter?

SCROOGE
Nothing. Nothing. There was a boy singing a Christmas Carol at my door last night. I should like to have given him something: that's all.

The Ghost smiles thoughtfully, waves its hand.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Let us see another Christmas!

Young Scrooge grows larger. The room darkens, becomes dirtier. Plaster falls, laths are shown. Young Scrooge is alone again, walking despairingly. Scrooge glances anxiously at the door.

It opens. A little girl, FAN, much younger than Young Scrooge, darts in, embracing and kissing him.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! To bring you home, home, home!

YOUNG SCROOGE
Home, little Fan?

FAN
Yes! Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever. Father is so much kinder than he used to be, that home's like Heaven! He spoke so gently to me one dear night when I was going to bed, that I was not afraid to ask him once more if you might come home; and he said Yes, you should; and sent me in a coach to bring you. And you're to be a man! And are never to come back here; but first, we're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

YOUNG SCROOGE
You are quite a woman, little Fan!

Fan claps her hands, laughs, and tries to touch his head. She stands on tiptoe to embrace him, then drags him towards the door.

A terrible voice in the hall cries, "Bring down Master Scrooge's box, there!" The SCHOOLMASTER appears, shakes Young Scrooge's hand with ferocious condescension. He leads the children to a cold parlor, offers them wine and cake. A servant offers "something" to the POSTBOY, who declines. Young Scrooge's trunk is tied to a chaise. The children bid the Schoolmaster goodbye and drive gaily away.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature, whom a breath might have withered. But she had a large heart!

SCROOGE
So she had. You're right. I will not gainsay it, Spirit. God forbid!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

Scrooge looks uneasy.

SCROOGE
Yes.

EXT. CITY STREETS - EVENING

They are now in the busy thoroughfares of a city, full of shadowy passengers, carts, and coaches. Shops are dressed for Christmas. The streets are lit.

The Ghost stops at a warehouse door.

SCROOGE
Know it! Was I apprenticed here!

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - EVENING

They enter. An old gentleman in a Welsh wig, MR. FEZZIWIG, sits behind a high desk.

SCROOGE
Why, it's old Fezziwig! Bless his heart; it's Fezziwig alive again!

Fezziwig lays down his pen, looks at the clock (seven o'clock). He rubs his hands, adjusts his waistcoat, and laughs.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick!

Young Scrooge, now a young man, enters briskly with his fellow-apprentice, DICK WILKINS.

SCROOGE
Dick Wilkins, to be sure! Bless me, yes. There he is. He was very much attached to me, was Dick. Poor Dick! Dear, dear!

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, my boys! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up, before a man can say Jack Robinson!

Young Scrooge and Dick charge into the street, put up the shutters, bar and pin them, and return, panting.

FEZZIWIG
Hilli-ho! Clear away, my lads, and let's have lots of room here! Hilli-ho, Dick! Chirrup, Ebenezer!

They clear away everything in a minute. The floor is swept, lamps trimmed, fire heaped. The warehouse becomes a snug, warm, dry, bright ballroom.

A FIDDLER enters, tunes his instrument. MRS. FEZZIWIG enters, a vast smile. The three MISS FEZZIWIGS, beaming. Six young followers. All the young men and women employed in the business. The HOUSEMAID with her cousin, the BAKER. The COOK with her brother's friend, the MILKMAN. A BOY from over the way, hiding behind a GIRL.

They all come in, some shyly, some boldly. Twenty couples dance. Old Fezziwig claps his hands to stop the dance.

FEZZIWIG
Well done!

The Fiddler plunges his face into a pot of porter, then instantly begins again. More dances, forfeits, cake, negus, cold roast and boiled meats, mince-pies, beer. The Fiddler strikes up "Sir Roger de Coverley." Old Fezziwig dances with Mrs. Fezziwig, full of agility and joy.

The clock strikes eleven. The ball breaks up. Mr. and Mrs. Fezziwig stand at the door, shaking hands with everyone, wishing them a Merry Christmas. They do the same for the two apprentices. The cheerful voices die away. The lads are left to their beds under a counter.

Scrooge has been engrossed in the scene. He remembers the Ghost, whose light burns clear.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude.

SCROOGE
Small!

The Spirit signs for him to listen to the apprentices, praising Fezziwig.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Why! Is it not? He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps. Is that so much that he deserves this praise?

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil. Say that his power lies in words and looks; in things so slight and insignificant that it is impossible to add and count 'em up: what then? The happiness he gives, is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

He feels the Spirit's glance and stops.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What is the matter?

SCROOGE
Nothing particular.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Something, I think?

SCROOGE
No. No. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

Young Scrooge turns down the lamps. Scrooge and the Ghost stand in the open air again.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
My time grows short. Quick!

INT. ANOTHER ROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge sees himself, older, in the prime of life, but with signs of care and avarice. He sits beside a fair young girl in a mourning-dress, BELLE, whose eyes are tearful.

BELLE
It matters little. To you, very little. Another idol has displaced me; and if it can cheer and comfort you in time to come, as I would have tried to do, I have no just cause to grieve.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

YOUNG SCROOGE
This is the even-handed dealing of the world! There is nothing on which it is so hard as poverty; and there is nothing it professes to condemn with such severity as the pursuit of wealth!

BELLE
You fear the world too much. All your other hopes have merged into the hope of being beyond the chance of its sordid reproach. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Have I not?

YOUNG SCROOGE
What then? Even if I have grown so much wiser, what then? I am not changed towards you.

Belle shakes her head.

YOUNG SCROOGE
Am I?

BELLE
Our contract is an old one. It was made when we were both poor and content to be so. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man.

YOUNG SCROOGE
I was a boy.

BELLE
Your own feeling tells you that you were not what you are. I am. That which promised happiness when we were one in heart, is fraught with misery now that we are two. It is enough that I have thought of it, and can release you.

YOUNG SCROOGE
Have I ever sought release?

BELLE
In words. No. Never.

YOUNG SCROOGE
In what, then?

BELLE
In a changed nature; in an altered spirit; in another atmosphere of life; another Hope as its great end. In everything that made my love of any worth or value in your sight. If this had never been between us, tell me, would you seek me out and try to win me now? Ah, no!

Young Scrooge struggles.

YOUNG SCROOGE
You think not.

BELLE
I would gladly think otherwise if I could, Heaven knows! But if you were free to-day, to-morrow, yesterday, can even I believe that you would choose a dowerless girl--you who, in your very confidence with her, weigh everything by Gain: or, choosing her, if for a moment you were false enough to your one guiding principle to do so, do I not know that your repentance and regret would surely follow? I do; and I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

Belle turns her head.

BELLE
You may--the memory of what is past half makes me hope you will--have pain in this. A very, very brief time, and you will dismiss the recollection of it, gladly, as an unprofitable dream. May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

She leaves him. They part.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

SCROOGE
No more! No more. I don't wish to see it. Show me no more!

The relentless Ghost pinions him, forcing him to watch.

INT. BELLE'S HOME - NIGHT

They are in another room, full of comfort. A beautiful young woman, BELLE (now a MATRON), sits near a fire with her daughter. The room is tumultuous with children. The mother and daughter laugh heartily. A knocking at the door. Belle, laughing, is borne towards it by the children. Her HUSBAND, MR. HAWKINS, enters, laden with Christmas toys. The children swarm him.

Scrooge watches the master of the house, his daughter leaning on him, sitting with his wife and daughter by the fire. Scrooge's sight grows dim, imagining such a creature calling him father.

MR. HAWKINS
Belle, I saw an old friend of yours this afternoon.

BELLE
Who was it?

MR. HAWKINS
Guess!

BELLE
(Laughing)
How can I? Tut, don't I know? Mr. Scrooge.

MR. HAWKINS
Mr. Scrooge it was. I passed his office window; and as it was not shut up, and he had a candle inside, I could scarcely help seeing him. His partner lies upon the point of death, I hear; and there he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

SCROOGE
(Broken voice)
Spirit! Remove me from this place.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I told you these were shadows of the things that have been. That they are what they are, do not blame me!

SCROOGE
Remove me! I cannot bear it!

Scrooge turns on the Ghost, seeing fragments of all the faces it has shown him. He wrestles with it.

SCROOGE
Leave me! Take me back. Haunt me no longer!

The Ghost's light burns high and bright. Scrooge seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down upon the Ghost's head. The Spirit drops beneath it, covered, but the light streams from under it.

Scrooge is exhausted, overcome by drowsiness. He is in his own bedroom. He gives the cap a parting squeeze, his hand relaxes. He reels to bed and falls into a heavy sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge awakes with a snore. He knows the bell is again upon the stroke of One. He puts aside his bed curtains, establishing a sharp look-out.

NARRATOR
Being prepared for almost anything, he was not by any means prepared for nothing.

The bell strikes One. No shape appears. Scrooge trembles. Five, ten, fifteen minutes pass. He lies in a blaze of ruddy light. He realizes the light might be from the adjoining room. He gets up softly and shuffles to the door.

A strange voice calls his name.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT (O.S.)
Come in!

Scrooge obeys.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

It is his own room, but transformed. Walls and ceiling hung with living green, gleaming berries. A mighty blaze roars up the chimney. Heaped on the floor, forming a throne, are turkeys, geese, game, meats, puddings, fruits, and seething bowls of punch.

On this couch sits a jolly GIANT, glorious to see, bearing a glowing torch like Plenty's horn. He holds it up, shedding light on Scrooge.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in! and know me better, man!

Scrooge enters timidly, hanging his head.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me!

Scrooge reverently does so. The Spirit is clothed in a simple green robe, bordered with white fur. Its capacious breast is bare. Its feet are bare. On its head, a holly wreath with icicles. Its dark brown curls are long and free. Girded round its middle is an antique scabbard, but no sword.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Have never walked forth with the younger members of my family; meaning (for I am very young) my elder brothers born in these later years?

SCROOGE
I don't think I have. I am afraid I have not. Have you had many brothers, Spirit?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
More than eighteen hundred.

SCROOGE
A tremendous family to provide for!

The Ghost of Christmas Present rises.

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. I went forth last night on compulsion, and I learnt a lesson which is working now. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge does as he is told, holding fast.

The room, the food, the fire, the glow, the hour, all vanish instantly.

EXT. CITY STREETS - CHRISTMAS MORNING

They stand in the city streets on Christmas morning. People shovel snow, making a brisk music. House fronts are black, windows blacker, contrasting with white snow. The sky is gloomy, streets choked with mist. Yet, an air of cheerfulness.

Jovial people call to one another, exchanging snowballs. Poulterers' and fruiterers' shops are radiant. Baskets of chestnuts, Spanish onions, pyramids of pears and apples, bunches of grapes, filberts. Gold and silver fish gasp in a bowl.

The Grocers' shops are nearly closed, but through the gaps, glimpses of scales, twine, canisters, scents of tea and coffee, raisins, almonds, cinnamon, spices, candied fruits. Customers tumble against each other, crashing baskets, making mistakes in good humor. The Grocer and his people are frank and fresh.

Church bells call people to church. Innumerable people carry their dinners to bakers' shops. The Spirit stands with Scrooge in a baker's doorway, sprinkling incense from his torch on their dinners. When angry words arise between jostling dinner-carriers, he sheds drops of water, restoring good humor.

SCROOGE
Is there a peculiar flavour in what you sprinkle from your torch?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There is. My own.

SCROOGE
Would it apply to any kind of dinner on this day?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

SCROOGE
Why to a poor one most?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Because it needs it most.

SCROOGE
Spirit, I wonder you, of all the beings in the many worlds about us, should desire to cramp these people's opportunities of innocent enjoyment.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I!

SCROOGE
You would deprive them of their means of dining every seventh day, often the only day on which they can be said to dine at all. Wouldn't you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I!

SCROOGE
You seek to close these places on the Seventh Day? And it comes to the same thing.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I seek!

SCROOGE
Forgive me if I am wrong. It has been done in your name, or at least in that of your family.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There are some upon this earth of yours who lay claim to know us, and who do their deeds of passion, pride, ill-will, hatred, envy, bigotry, and selfishness in our name, who are as strange to us and all our kith and kin, as if they had never lived. Remember that, and charge their doings on themselves, not us.

Scrooge promises he will. They go on, invisible, into the suburbs.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - CHRISTMAS DAY

The Ghost leads Scrooge to Bob Cratchit's four-roomed house. The Spirit smiles and blesses the dwelling with a sprinkling of his torch.

MRS. CRATCHIT, poorly dressed but brave in ribbons, lays the cloth, assisted by BELINDA CRATCHIT, her daughter, also in ribbons. MASTER PETER CRATCHIT, wearing Bob's monstrous shirt collar, plunges a fork into potatoes. Two smaller YOUNG CRATCHITS, a boy and a girl, tear in, screaming they smelled the goose. They dance about the table, exalting Peter, who blows the fire.

MRS. CRATCHIT
What has ever got your precious father then? And your brother, Tiny Tim! And Martha warn't as late last Christmas Day by half-an-hour?

MARTHA CRATCHIT, a girl, appears.

MARTHA CRATCHIT
Here's Martha, mother!

YOUNG CRATCHITS
Here's Martha, mother! Hurrah! There's such a goose, Martha!

MRS. CRATCHIT
Why, bless your heart alive, my dear, how late you are!

She kisses Martha a dozen times, taking off her shawl and bonnet.

MARTHA CRATCHIT
We'd a deal of work to finish up last night, and had to clear away this morning, mother!

MRS. CRATCHIT
Well! Never mind so long as you are come. Sit ye down before the fire, my dear, and have a warm, Lord bless ye!

YOUNG CRATCHITS
No, no! There's father coming! Hide, Martha, hide!

Martha hides. BOB CRATCHIT enters, wearing a long comforter, his threadbare clothes darned. TINY TIM, bearing a little crutch and an iron frame on his leg, is on Bob's shoulder.

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, where's our Martha?

MRS. CRATCHIT
Not coming.

BOB CRATCHIT
Not coming! Upon Christmas Day!

Martha comes out from behind the closet door and runs into his arms. The Young Cratchits hustle Tiny Tim into the wash-house to hear the pudding singing.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. Somehow he gets thoughtful, sitting by himself so much, and thinks the strangest things you ever heard. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day, who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

Bob's voice trembles. Tiny Tim's crutch is heard. He returns, escorted by his siblings, to his stool by the fire. Bob, turning up his cuffs, compounds a hot mixture in a jug. Peter and the Young Cratchits fetch the goose.

A bustle ensues. Mrs. Cratchit makes gravy. Peter mashes potatoes. Belinda sweetens apple-sauce. Martha dusts plates. Bob seats Tiny Tim. The Young Cratchits cram spoons into their mouths. Grace is said. Mrs. Cratchit prepares to carve the goose. A gush of stuffing issues forth. A murmur of delight. Tiny Tim beats the table with his knife handle.

TINY TIM
Hurrah!

Bob says there never was such a goose. It is a sufficient dinner. Mrs. Cratchit, surveying a small bone, says they hadn't eaten it all. Belinda changes the plates. Mrs. Cratchit leaves to fetch the pudding.

NARRATOR
All sorts of horrors were supposed. Suppose it should not be done enough! Suppose it should break in turning out! Suppose somebody should have got over the wall of the back-yard, and stolen it!

A great deal of steam. A smell like washing-day, an eating-house, a pastrycook's, and a laundress's. Mrs. Cratchit enters, flushed but smiling proudly, with the pudding, a speckled cannon-ball, blazing with ignited brandy and holly.

Bob says it is Mrs. Cratchit's greatest success. Mrs. Cratchit confesses doubts about the flour. Everyone praises it.

The dinner is done. The cloth cleared, hearth swept, fire made up. The hot mixture is tasted. Apples and oranges on the table, chestnuts on the fire. The Cratchit family draws round the hearth. Bob's elbow displays two tumblers and a custard-cup. Bob serves the hot mixture. Chestnuts sputter.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

ALL CRATCHITS
God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Tiny Tim sits close to his father. Bob holds his withered hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head, overcome with penitence.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Man, if man you be in heart, not adamant, forbear that wicked cant until you have discovered What the surplus is, and Where it is. Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die? Oh God! to hear the Insect on the leaf pronouncing on the too much life among his hungry brothers in the dust!

Scrooge bends before the rebuke. He hears his name.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon, and I hope he'd have a good appetite for it.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, the children! Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
It should be Christmas Day, I am sure, on which one drinks the health of such an odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man as Mr. Scrooge. You know he is, Robert! Nobody knows it better than you do, poor fellow!

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year! He'll be very merry and very happy, I have no doubt!

The children drink the toast without heartiness. Tiny Tim drinks last. Scrooge's name casts a dark shadow.

After five minutes, they are ten times merrier. Bob tells them of a situation for Peter. The Young Cratchits laugh. Peter looks thoughtfully at the fire. Martha tells of her work as a milliner's apprentice, and seeing a countess and a lord. Peter pulls up his collars. Chestnuts and the jug go round. Tiny Tim sings a song about a lost child.

NARRATOR
They were happy, grateful, pleased with one another, and contented with the time.

They fade. Scrooge's eye is on Tiny Tim until the last.

EXT. CITY STREETS - NIGHT

It is getting dark and snowing heavily. Scrooge and the Spirit go along the streets. Fires roar in kitchens and parlors. Children run out to meet family. Shadows of guests assemble. Girls trip off to a neighbor's house.

NARRATOR
Blessings on it, how the Ghost exulted! How it bared its breadth of breast, and opened its capacious palm, and floated on, outpouring, with a generous hand, its bright and harmless mirth on everything within its reach!

A LAMPLIGHTER laughs as the Spirit passes.

EXT. BLEAK MOOR - NIGHT

Without warning, they stand on a bleak, desert moor. Monstrous stones, moss, furze, coarse grass. The setting sun leaves a streak of fiery red.

SCROOGE
What place is this?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
A place where Miners live, who labour in the bowels of the earth. But they know me. See!

A light shines from a hut window. They pass through the wall. An old man and woman, with children and grandchildren, are gathered round a glowing fire, gaily dressed. The old man sings a Christmas song. They all join in the chorus.

The Spirit bids Scrooge hold his robe. They speed above the moor.

EXT. HEAVING SEA - NIGHT

Scrooge sees the last of the land, a frightful range of rocks. His ears are deafened by thundering water. A solitary lighthouse stands on a dismal reef. Two men watch the light, make a fire, wish each other Merry Christmas in their grog, and sing a sturdy song.

The Ghost speeds on, above the black and heaving sea. They light on a ship. They stand beside the HELMSMAN, the LOOK-OUT, the OFFICERS. Every man hums a Christmas tune or thinks of a bygone Christmas Day. Every man has a kinder word for another.

Scrooge hears a hearty laugh. It is his nephew's.

INT. FRED'S HOME - NIGHT

They are in a bright, dry, gleaming room. The Spirit stands smiling. Fred laughs, holding his sides. FRED'S WIFE laughs as heartily. Their friends roar.

FRED
Ha, ha! Ha, ha, ha, ha! He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

FRED'S WIFE
More shame for him, Fred!

Fred's wife is pretty, with a dimpled face and sunny eyes.

FRED
He's a comical old fellow, that's the truth: and not so pleasant as he might be. However, his offences carry their own punishment, and I have nothing to say against him.

FRED'S WIFE
I'm sure he is very rich, Fred. At least you always tell me so.

FRED
What of that, my dear! His wealth is of no use to him. He don't do any good with it. He don't make himself comfortable with it. He hasn't the satisfaction of thinking--ha, ha, ha!--that he is ever going to benefit US with it.

FRED'S WIFE
I have no patience with him.

Fred's wife's SISTERS and other LADIES express the same opinion.

FRED
Oh, I have! I am sorry for him; I couldn't be angry with him if I tried. Who suffers by his ill whims! Himself, always. Here, he takes it into his head to dislike us, and he won't come and dine with us. What's the consequence? He don't lose much of a dinner.

FRED'S WIFE
Indeed, I think he loses a very good dinner.

Everyone agrees. They are clustered round the fire, by lamplight, with dessert.

FRED
Well! I'm very glad to hear it, because I haven't great faith in these young housekeepers. What do you say, Topper?

TOPPER, who has his eye on one of Fred's wife's sisters, replies that a bachelor has no right to an opinion. MISS FANSHAWE blushes.

FRED'S WIFE
Do go on, Fred. He never finishes what he begins to say! He is such a ridiculous fellow!

Fred laughs again. Miss Fanshawe tries to ward off the infection with aromatic vinegar, but everyone follows his example.

FRED
I was only going to say that the consequence of his taking a dislike to us, and not making merry with us, is, as I think, that he loses some pleasant moments, which could do him no harm. I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not, for I pity him. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it--I defy him--if he finds me going there, in good temper, year after year, and saying Uncle Scrooge, how are you? If it only puts him in the vein to leave his poor clerk fifty pounds, that's something; and I think I shook him yesterday.

They laugh at the notion of shaking Scrooge. After tea, they have music. Fred's wife plays the harp, a simple air familiar to Young Scrooge. Scrooge softens, thinking he might have cultivated kindness years ago.

They play forfeits. Blind-man's buff. TOPPER, pretending to be blind, always finds Miss Fanshawe. He pretends not to know her, touching her head-dress, ring, and chain.

Scrooge's niece sits in a large chair. The Ghost and Scrooge are behind her. She joins in forfeits and games. Scrooge, forgetting he is unheard, guesses answers loudly.

The Ghost is pleased. Scrooge begs to stay until the guests depart. The Spirit says it cannot be done.

SCROOGE
Here is a new game. One half hour, Spirit, only one!

They play "Yes and No." Fred thinks of an animal. The questions reveal it is a live, disagreeable, savage animal that growls, grunts, and talks, lives in London, walks the streets, isn't a show, isn't led, isn't in a menagerie, isn't killed in a market, and isn't a horse, ass, cow, bull, tiger, dog, pig, cat, or bear. Fred roars with laughter. Miss Fanshawe cries out:

MISS FANSHAWE
I have found it out! I know what it is, Fred! I know what it is!

FRED
What is it?

MISS FANSHAWE
It's your Uncle Scro-o-o-o-oge!

It certainly is. Everyone laughs.

FRED
He has given us plenty of merriment, I am sure, and it would be ungrateful not to drink his health. Here is a glass of mulled wine ready to our hand at the moment; and I say, 'Uncle Scrooge!'

ALL
Well! Uncle Scrooge!

FRED
A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! He wouldn't take it from me, but may he have it, nevertheless. Uncle Scrooge!

Scrooge has become gay and light of heart. He would have pledged them in return, but the scene passes off with Fred's last word. He and the Spirit are again on their travels.

NARRATOR
Much they saw, and far they went, and many homes they visited, but always with a happy end. The Spirit stood beside sick beds, and they were cheerful; on foreign lands, and they were close at home; by struggling men, and they were patient in their greater hope; by poverty, and it was rich.

It is a long night. The Ghost grows older, its hair grey.

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe, is very brief. It ends to-night.

SCROOGE
To-night!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To-night at midnight. Hark! The time is drawing near.

Chimes ring the three quarters past eleven.

SCROOGE
Forgive me if I am not justified in what I ask, but I see something strange, and not belonging to yourself, protruding from your skirts. Is it a foot or a claw?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
It might be a claw, for the flesh there is upon it. Look here.

From the folds of its robe, it brings two children, wretched, abject, frightful. They kneel at its feet, clinging to its garment.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Oh, Man! look here. Look, look, down here!

They are a boy and girl. Yellow, meagre, ragged, scowling, wolfish, but prostrate in humility.

Scrooge starts back, appalled. He tries to say they are fine children, but the words choke.

SCROOGE
Spirit! are they yours?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. And they cling to me, appealing from their fathers. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased. Deny it! Slander those who tell it ye! Admit it for your factious purposes, and make it worse. And bide the end!

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. Scrooge looks for the Ghost, but sees it not. As the last stroke ceases, he remembers Marley's prediction. He lifts his eyes. A solemn PHANTOM, draped and hooded, comes towards him like a mist.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The PHANTOM, the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME, slowly, gravely, silently approaches. Scrooge bends to his knee. It is shrouded in a deep black garment, concealing its head, face, and form, leaving only one outstretched hand visible.

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come?

The Spirit points onward.

SCROOGE
You are about to show me shadows of the things that have not happened, but will happen in the time before us. Is that so, Spirit?

The garment contracts, as if the Spirit inclined its head. Scrooge's legs tremble. The Spirit pauses.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But as I know your purpose is to do me good, and as I hope to live to be another man from what I was, I am prepared to bear you company, and do it with a thankful heart. Will you not speak to me?

No reply. The hand points straight before them.

SCROOGE
Lead on! Lead on! The night is waning fast, and it is precious time to me, I know. Lead on, Spirit!

The Phantom moves away. Scrooge follows in its shadow.

EXT. 'CHANGE - DAY

They are on 'Change, amongst merchants.

NARRATOR
The city seemed to spring up about them. Merchants hurried up and down, chinked money, conversed in groups, looked at watches.

The Spirit stops beside a knot of businessmen. Scrooge advances to listen.

MR. PIMPLE
No, I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

MR. FENWICK
When did he die?

MR. PIMPLE
Last night, I believe.

MR. GRUBB
Why, what was the matter with him? I thought he'd never die.

MR. PIMPLE
God knows.

MR. GOBBLER
What has he done with his money?

MR. PIMPLE
I haven't heard. Left it to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me. That's all I know.

A general laugh.

MR. PIMPLE
It's likely to be a very cheap funeral; for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it. Suppose we make up a party and volunteer?

MR. GOBBLER
I don't mind going if a lunch is provided. But I must be fed, if I make one.

Another laugh.

MR. PIMPLE
Well, I am the most disinterested among you, after all, for I never wear black gloves, and I never eat lunch. But I'll offer to go, if anybody else will. When I come to think of it, I'm not at all sure that I wasn't his most particular friend; for we used to stop and speak whenever we met. Bye, bye!

The men stroll away. Scrooge knows them. He looks to the Spirit for explanation.

EXT. STREET - DAY

The Phantom glides into a street. Its finger points to two men meeting. Scrooge listens. He knows these men, wealthy businessmen.

MR. DILBER
How are you?

MR. SNOUT
How are you?

MR. DILBER
Well! Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

MR. SNOUT
So I am told. Cold, isn't it?

MR. DILBER
Seasonable for Christmas time. You're not a skater, I suppose?

MR. SNOUT
No. No. Something else to think of. Good morning!

They part. Scrooge is surprised by the triviality, but believes there is a hidden purpose. He looks for his own image, but another man stands in his accustomed corner.

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT

They enter an obscure, foul part of town. A low-browed, beetling shop, where iron, old rags, bottles, bones, and greasy offal are bought. Heaps of rusty keys, chains, ledgers. An old, grey-haired rascal, OLD JOE, sits by a charcoal stove, smoking his pipe.

A woman with a heavy bundle, MRS. DILBER, slinks in. Another woman, MRS. GRUBB, similarly laden, follows. Then MR. TOMLINSON, in faded black. They are startled to see each other. They all burst into laughter.

MRS. DILBER
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! Let the laundress alone to be the second; and let the undertaker's man alone to be the third. Look here, old Joe, here's a chance! If we haven't all three met here without meaning it!

OLD JOE
You couldn't have met in a better place. Come into the parlour. You were made free of it long ago, you know; and the other two an't strangers. Stop till I shut the door of the shop. Ah! How it skreeks! There an't such a rusty bit of metal in the place as its own hinges, I believe; and I'm sure there's no such old bones here, as mine. Ha, ha! We're all suitable to our calling, we're well matched. Come into the parlour. Come into the parlour.

The parlor is behind a screen of rags. Old Joe rakes the fire, trims his smoky lamp. Mrs. Dilber throws her bundle on the floor, sits on a stool, looking defiantly at the others.

MRS. DILBER
What odds then! What odds, Mrs. Dilber? Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did.

MRS. GRUBB
That's true, indeed! No man more so.

MRS. DILBER
Why then, don't stand staring as if you was afraid, woman; who's the wiser? We're not going to pick holes in each other's coats, I suppose?

MRS. GRUBB
^ No, indeed!
MR. TOMLINSON
^ We should hope not.

MRS. DILBER
Very well, then! That's enough. Who's the worse for the loss of a few things like these? Not a dead man, I suppose.

MRS. GRUBB
(Laughing)
No, indeed.

MRS. DILBER
If he wanted to keep 'em after he was dead, a wicked old screw, why wasn't he natural in his lifetime? If he had been, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death, instead of lying gasping out his last there, alone by himself.

MRS. GRUBB
It's the truest word that ever was spoke. It's a judgment on him.

MRS. DILBER
I wish it was a little heavier judgment; and it should have been, you may depend upon it, if I could have laid my hands on anything else. Open that bundle, old Joe, and let me know the value of it. Speak out plain. I'm not afraid to be the first, nor afraid for them to see it. We know pretty well that we were helping ourselves, before we met here, I believe. It's no sin. Open the bundle, Joe.

Mr. Tomlinson produces his plunder: a seal or two, a pencil-case, sleeve-buttons, a brooch. Old Joe examines and appraises them, chalking sums on the wall.

OLD JOE
That's your account, and I wouldn't give another sixpence, if I was to be boiled for not doing it. Who's next?

Mrs. Grubb is next: sheets, towels, wearing apparel, silver teaspoons, sugar-tongs, boots. Her account is stated on the wall.

OLD JOE
I always give too much to ladies. It's a weakness of mine, and that's the way I ruin myself. That's your account. If you asked me for another penny, and made it an open question, I'd repent of being so liberal and knock off half-a-crown.

MRS. DILBER
And now undo my bundle, Joe.

Joe kneels, unfastens knots, and drags out a large, heavy roll of dark stuff.

OLD JOE
What do you call this? Bed-curtains!

MRS. DILBER
(Laughing)
Ah! Bed-curtains!

OLD JOE
You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

MRS. DILBER
Yes I do. Why not?

OLD JOE
You were born to make your fortune, and you'll certainly do it.

MRS. DILBER
I certainly shan't hold my hand, when I can get anything in it by reaching it out, for the sake of such a man as He was, I promise you, Joe. Don't drop that oil upon the blankets, now.

OLD JOE
His blankets?

MRS. DILBER
Whose else's do you think? He isn't likely to take cold without 'em, I dare say.

OLD JOE
I hope he didn't die of anything catching? Eh?

MRS. DILBER
Don't you be afraid of that. I an't so fond of his company that I'd loiter about him for such things, if he did. Ah! you may look through that shirt till your eyes ache; but you won't find a hole in it, nor a threadbare place. It's the best he had, and a fine one too. They'd have wasted it, if it hadn't been for me.

OLD JOE
What do you call wasting of it?

MRS. DILBER
Putting it on him to be buried in, to be sure. Somebody was fool enough to do it, but I took it off again. If calico an't good enough for such a purpose, it isn't good enough for anything. It's quite as becoming to the body. He can't look uglier than he did in that one.

Scrooge listens in horror. They sit grouped about their spoil. Old Joe produces a flannel bag with money.

MRS. DILBER
(Laughing)
Ha, ha! This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

SCROOGE
(Shuddering)
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

He recoils. The scene changes.

INT. DARK BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge almost touches a bare, uncurtained bed. Beneath a ragged sheet, a covered form lies. The room is very dark. A pale light falls on the bed, revealing the unwatched, unwept, uncared-for body of a man.

Scrooge glances at the Phantom. Its hand points to the head. The cover is carelessly adjusted. Scrooge longs to lift it, but cannot.

NARRATOR
Oh cold, cold, rigid, dreadful Death, set up thine altar here, and dress it with such terrors as thou hast at thy command: for this is thy dominion! But of the loved, revered, and honoured head, thou canst not turn one hair to thy dread purposes, or make one feature odious.

Scrooge hears no voice, yet he hears these words. He thinks of the man's foremost thoughts if raised: avarice, hard-dealing. A cat tears at the door. Rats gnaw beneath the hearth-stone.

SCROOGE
Spirit! This is a fearful place. In leaving it, I shall not leave its lesson, trust me. Let us go!

The Ghost still points to the head.

SCROOGE
I understand you, and I would do it, if I could. But I have not the power, Spirit. I have not the power.

The Ghost seems to look at him.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town, who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

The Phantom spreads its dark robe like a wing, then withdraws it.

INT. CAROLINE'S HOME - DAY

A room by daylight. CAROLINE, a mother, walks up and down, anxious. She starts at every sound, looks out the window, glances at the clock, tries to work with her needle. Her children play.

A knock. She hurries to the door, meets her HUSBAND. His face is careworn, but now has a serious delight he struggles to repress. He sits to dinner. Caroline asks for news.

CAROLINE
Is it good? Or bad?

HUSBAND
Bad.

CAROLINE
We are quite ruined?

HUSBAND
No. There is hope yet, Caroline.

CAROLINE
If he relents, there is! Nothing is past hope, if such a miracle has happened.

HUSBAND
He is past relenting. He is dead.

Caroline, a mild and patient creature, is thankful in her soul. She clasps her hands. She prays forgiveness, but the first was her heart's emotion.

HUSBAND
What the half-drunken woman whom I told you of last night, said to me, when I tried to see him and obtain a week's delay; and what I thought was a mere excuse to avoid me; turns out to have been quite true. He was not only very ill, but dying, then.

CAROLINE
To whom will our debt be transferred?

HUSBAND
I don't know. But before that time we shall be ready with the money; and even though we were not, it would be a bad fortune indeed to find so merciless a creditor in his successor. We may sleep to-night with light hearts, Caroline!

Their hearts are lighter. The children's faces are brighter. It is a happier house for this man's death. The Ghost shows Scrooge only pleasure.

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death; or that dark chamber, Spirit, which we left just now, will be for ever present to me.

EXT. CITY STREETS - NIGHT

The Ghost conducts him through familiar streets. Scrooge looks for himself, but is nowhere to be seen.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - NIGHT

They enter Bob Cratchit's house. The mother and children are seated round the fire, quiet. The Young Cratchits are still. Peter has a book. Mrs. Cratchit and her daughters sew.

PETER CRATCHIT
"And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them."

Scrooge wonders where he heard those words. Mrs. Cratchit lays her work down, puts her hand to her face.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes.

NARRATOR
The colour? Ah, poor Tiny Tim!

MRS. CRATCHIT
They're better now again. It makes them weak by candle-light; and I wouldn't show weak eyes to your father when he comes home, for the world. It must be near his time.

PETER CRATCHIT
Past it rather. But I think he has walked a little slower than he used, these few last evenings, mother.

They are quiet again.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with--I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed.

PETER CRATCHIT
And so have I. Often.

BELINDA CRATCHIT
And so have I.

ALL CRATCHITS
So had all.

MRS. CRATCHIT
But he was very light to carry, and his father loved him so, that it was no trouble: no trouble. And there is your father at the door!

She hurries out. Bob Cratchit enters, wearing his comforter. His tea is ready. The Young Cratchits get on his knees, laying their cheeks against his face.

Bob is cheerful. He praises Mrs. Cratchit and the girls' work.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Sunday! You went to-day, then, Robert?

BOB CRATCHIT
Yes, my dear. I wish you could have gone. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is. But you'll see it often. I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child! My little child!

He breaks down, then leaves the room.

INT. TINY TIM'S ROOM - NIGHT

Bob goes upstairs to a cheerfully lit room, hung with Christmas decorations. A chair is by the child's bed. Bob sits, composes himself, and kisses Tiny Tim's little face. He is reconciled. He goes downstairs.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - NIGHT

They draw about the fire, talking. The girls and mother work. Bob tells them of Fred's kindness.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Knew what, my dear?

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, that you were a good wife.

PETER CRATCHIT
Everybody knows that!

BOB CRATCHIT
Very well observed, my boy! I hope they do. 'Heartily sorry,' he said, 'for your good wife. If I can be of service to you in any way,' he said, giving me his card, 'that's where I live. Pray come to me.' Now, it wasn't for the sake of anything he might be able to do for us, so much as for his kind way, that this was quite delightful. It really seemed as if he had known our Tiny Tim, and felt with us.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'm sure he's a good soul!

BOB CRATCHIT
You would be surer of it, my dear, if you saw and spoke to him. I shouldn't be at all surprised--mark what I say!--if he got Peter a better situation.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Only hear that, Peter.

BETSY CRATCHIT
And then Peter will be keeping company with some one, and setting up for himself.

PETER CRATCHIT
(Grinning)
Get along with you!

BOB CRATCHIT
It's just as likely as not, one of these days; though there's plenty of time for that, my dear. But however and whenever we part from one another, I am sure we shall none of us forget poor Tiny Tim--shall we--or this first parting that there was among us?

ALL CRATCHITS
Never, father!

BOB CRATCHIT
And I know, my dears, that when we recollect how patient and how mild he was; although he was a little, little child; we shall not quarrel easily among ourselves, and forget poor Tiny Tim in doing it.

ALL CRATCHITS
No, never, father!

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very happy. I am very happy!

Mrs. Cratchit, his daughters, and the Young Cratchits kiss him. Peter and Bob shake hands.

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. I know it, but I know not how. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead?

The Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come conveys him to the resorts of businessmen, but shows him not himself.

EXT. 'CHANGE - DAY

Scrooge sees his office, but it is not his. The furniture is different, the figure in the chair is not him. The Phantom points away.

SCROOGE
This court, through which we hurry now, is where my place of occupation is. I see the house. Let me behold what I shall be, in days to come! The house is yonder. Why do you point away?

The inexorable finger does not change. Scrooge hastens to his office window, looks in. It is an office, but not his. The Phantom points as before.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - DAY

Scrooge follows the Ghost to an iron gate. A churchyard, walled by houses, overrun with grass and weeds. The Spirit stands among the graves, pointing to one. Scrooge advances, trembling.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The Ghost points downward to the grave.

SCROOGE
Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

The Spirit is immovable. Scrooge creeps forward, trembling, and reads the name on the neglected grave: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
(On his knees)
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to him, and back again.

SCROOGE
No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

The finger is still there.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope!

For the first time, the hand appears to shake.

SCROOGE
(Falling to the ground)
Good Spirit, Your nature intercedes for me, and pities me. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life!

The kind hand trembles.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. I will not shut out the lessons that they teach. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

In his agony, he catches the spectral hand. It seeks to free itself, but he holds it. The Spirit, stronger, repulses him. Holding up his hands in a last prayer, he sees an alteration in the Phantom's hood and dress. It shrinks, collapses, and dwindles down into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Scrooge is in his own bed. The bedpost is his own. The bed is his own. The room is his own.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this! I say it on my knees, old Jacob; on my knees!

He is fluttered and glowing, his voice broken. His face is wet with tears.

SCROOGE
They are not torn down! They are not torn down, rings and all. They are here--I am here--the shadows of the things that would have been, may be dispelled. They will be. I know they will!

He fumbles with his garments, turning them inside out, putting them on upside down.

SCROOGE
I don't know what to do! I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. I am as giddy as a drunken man. A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world. Hallo here! Whoop! Hallo!

He frisks into the sitting-room, winded.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - CHRISTMAS MORNING

SCROOGE
There's the saucepan that the gruel was in! There's the door, by which the Ghost of Jacob Marley entered! There's the corner where the Ghost of Christmas Present, sat! There's the window where I saw the wandering Spirits! It's all right, it's all true, it all happened. Ha ha ha!

He laughs, a splendid, illustrious laugh.

SCROOGE
I don't know what day of the month it is! I don't know how long I've been among the Spirits. I don't know anything. I'm quite a baby. Never mind. I don't care. I'd rather be a baby. Hallo! Whoop! Hallo here!

Church bells ring out lustily. He runs to the window, opens it, and puts out his head. No fog, no mist. Clear, bright, jovial, stirring, cold. Golden sunlight, heavenly sky, sweet fresh air, merry bells.

SCROOGE
What's to-day!

TIMOTHY in Sunday clothes looks up.

TIMOTHY
EH?

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

TIMOTHY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
(To himself)
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. They can do anything they like. Of course they can. Of course they can. Hallo, my fine fellow!

TIMOTHY
Hallo!

SCROOGE
Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner?

TIMOTHY
I should hope I did.

SCROOGE
An intelligent boy! A remarkable boy! Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there?--Not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

TIMOTHY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! It's a pleasure to talk to him. Yes, my buck!

TIMOTHY
It's hanging there now.

SCROOGE
Is it? Go and buy it.

TIMOTHY
Walk-ER!

SCROOGE
No, no, I am in earnest. Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here, that I may give them the direction where to take it. Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

Timothy is off like a shot.

SCROOGE
(Whispering, rubbing his hands)
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim. Joe Miller never made such a joke as sending it to Bob's will be!

He writes the address with a shaky hand, then goes downstairs to open the street door.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Scrooge stands by the door. The knocker catches his eye.

SCROOGE
I shall love it, as long as I live! I scarcely ever looked at it before. What an honest expression it has in its face! It's a wonderful knocker!--Here's the Turkey! Hallo! Whoop! How are you! Merry Christmas!

MR. JENKINS arrives with the enormous TURKEY, followed by Timothy.

SCROOGE
Why, it's impossible to carry that to Camden Town. You must have a cab.

Scrooge chuckles as he pays for the Turkey, the cab, and recompenses Timothy. He sits breathless in his chair, chuckling till he cries.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Scrooge shaves, his hand still shaking. He dresses in his best clothes.

EXT. CITY STREETS - CHRISTMAS MORNING

He walks the streets, regarding everyone with a delighted smile. Three or four good-humored fellows say, "Good morning, sir! A merry Christmas to you!" Scrooge finds them the blithest sounds he has ever heard.

He sees MR. WITHERS approaching. A pang crosses his heart. He quickens his pace, takes the old gentleman by both hands.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, How do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. A merry Christmas to you, sir!

MR. WITHERS
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Yes, that is my name, and I fear it may not be pleasant to you. Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness--

Scrooge whispers in his ear.

MR. WITHERS
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Will you do me that favour?

MR. WITHERS
My dear sir, I don't know what to say to such munifi--

SCROOGE
Don't say anything, please. Come and see me. Will you come and see me?

MR. WITHERS
I will!

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. I am much obliged to you. I thank you fifty times. Bless you!

Scrooge goes to church, walks about the streets, pats children, questions beggars, looks into kitchens. Everything yields him pleasure.

INT. FRED'S HOME - CHRISTMAS DAY

In the afternoon, he turns to Fred's house. He passes the door a dozen times before knocking. POLLY answers.

SCROOGE
Is your master at home, my dear?

POLLY
Yes, sir.

SCROOGE
Where is he, my love?

POLLY
He's in the dining-room, sir, along with mistress. I'll show you up-stairs, if I please.

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. He knows me. I'll go in here, my dear.

He turns the dining-room lock gently, sidles his face in. Fred and his wife are looking at the table.

SCROOGE
Fred!

Fred's wife starts.

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred nearly shakes his arm off. Scrooge is at home in five minutes. The party is wonderful.

INT. SCROOGE & MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - MORNING

Next morning, Scrooge is early at the office. He wants to catch Bob Cratchit coming late. The clock strikes nine. No Bob. A quarter past. No Bob. Bob is eighteen minutes and a half late. Scrooge sits with his door wide open.

Bob enters, hat off, comforter off. He is on his stool in a jiffy, writing furiously.

SCROOGE
(Growling, feigning his old voice)
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Yes. I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

Bob appears from the Tank.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend, I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore--

Scrooge leaps from his stool, gives Bob a dig in the waistcoat that sends him staggering back into the Tank.

SCROOGE
--and therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob trembles, eyes the ruler.

SCROOGE
(With earnestness, clapping Bob on the back)
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you, for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon, over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

NARRATOR
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man, as the good old city knew. Some people laughed to see the alteration in him, but he let them laugh, and little heeded them; for he was wise enough to know that nothing ever happened on this globe, for good, at which some people did not have their fill of laughter in the outset. His own heart laughed: and that was quite enough for him. He had no further intercourse with Spirits, but lived upon the Total Abstinence Principle, ever afterwards; and it was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge. May that be truly said of us, and all of us! And so, as Tiny Tim observed, God bless Us, Every One!

FADE OUT.
